{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And if it is a field of his purchase, etc.</b> There is a distinction between a purchased field and an ancestral field: a purchased field is not divided to the priests in the Jubilee, because he cannot consecrate it beyond the Jubilee — for in the Jubilee it was destined to leave his hand and return to the owners. Therefore, if he comes to redeem it, he redeems it with the same fixed valuation money of an ancestral field; and if he does not redeem it, and the treasurer sells it to another — or if he himself redeems it — <b>in the year of the Jubilee the field shall return to him from whom he bought it</b> — to the one who consecrated it. Lest you say 'to him from whom he bought it' means this last purchaser who bought it from him — and that would be the treasurer — therefore Scripture needed to say <b>to him to whom the possession of the land belongs</b> — by inheritance from the fathers, and that is the original owners who sold it to the one who consecrated it (Arakhin 26)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:08:59.961514376Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}